In a Linux application, request.args['file'] is untrusted. /srv/exports contains public exports, while the process can also read confidential files outside it. No confinement checks or symlink protections are performed. Review file access:
path = os.path.join('/srv/exports', request.args['file'])
return open(path, 'rb').read()

Review access control. A verified session identifies the caller, but invoices belong to different users. Invoice IDs are random UUIDs that sometimes appear in shared logs. This is the complete data-access check:
@login_required
def invoice(invoice_id):
    row = db.execute('SELECT pdf FROM invoices WHERE id=?', (invoice_id,)).fetchone()
    if row is None: abort(404)
    return row['pdf']

A download handler uses this exact map. Unknown keys return 404. Both files are deliberately public, deployment-owned regular files; users cannot modify the directory or introduce symlinks. Review path traversal only:
FILES = {'guide': '/srv/public/guide.pdf', 'terms': '/srv/public/terms.pdf'}
path = FILES.get(request.args.get('doc', ''))
if path is None: abort(404)
return send_file(path)

Is this path-traversal patch sufficient? All paths below are POSIX paths; the filesystem is static during the request and contains no symlinks. Only descendants of /srv/reports are allowed. The process can also read /srv/reports-old/private.txt. relative is arbitrary client input:
root = Path('/srv/reports')
target = (root / relative).resolve()
if not str(target).startswith(str(root)): abort(403)
return target.read_bytes()

A customer message is stored verbatim and later displayed to staff. The page has no sanitizer, Trusted Types enforcement, or restrictive CSP. #message is a div. Review this display operation:
document.querySelector('#message').innerHTML = message.body;
A teammate argues it is safe because scripts inserted through innerHTML do not execute as ordinary script elements.
